#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""codex-human-transcript — fast, colorful TUI over Codex rollout NDJSON transcripts.

Interactive mode (default on a tty): incremental NDJSON tailing with a per-file
render cache, ANSI-color rendering inside curses, vim-style keys, search, mouse
wheel, and dirty-flag redraws so it stays snappy in tmux over bad links.
Plain mode (--plain / piped / --once) keeps the original stream-to-stdout flow.

Text logs: every mode can mirror sessions into plain-text files (no JSON, no
ANSI) that humans and agents can `tail -F`. `--mirror` does only that, headless:

    codex-human-transcript --mirror &
    tail -F ~/.local/state/codex-human-transcript/logs/latest.log

Roots: Codex sessions dirs ($CODEX_HOME, ~/.codex, WSL-mounted Windows profiles) and
Claude Code project dirs ($CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, ~/.claude/projects, WSL profiles) are both
auto-discovered. Press `r` in the TUI to pick which roots to watch; the selection is saved
in ~/.config/codex-human-transcript/config.json. Sessions from both agents share one list,
tagged CX / CL. Press `t` to hide Claude thinking blocks (text logs always keep them).

Status: Codex sessions read their state from Codex's own thread database beside the sessions
dir (a turn in flight stays "working" however long its rollout grows) and take their names from
it; Claude sessions are read from the transcript, with `/slash` commands ignored and names from
history.jsonl. A "working" session nobody wrote to for an hour shows as stale. Press `a` (or pass
`--active`) to hide idle sessions older than that window; `--active-window 30m` tunes it.

Token usage: sessions are tallied from the transcript itself (Codex `token_count` events,
Claude per-message `usage`), so watching costs the agent nothing. `--mirror` tallies every
session it mirrors; the TUI tallies the sessions you open and shows earlier totals from the ledger.
The footer shows `Σ ↑input ⇄cache-hit% ↓output · requests · tools · errors · ctx`, the row above
it shows the current `⚙ model · effort`, the agent list carries both per session, and one
JSON per session lands in ~/.local/state/codex-human-transcript/ledger/ for comparing
sessions over time (`jq -s . ledger/*.json`). `--no-ledger` turns the files off.
"""

